# The nameplate amounts whose unit carries a period. One row per layer whose supply is quoted as
# a rate (`4 GPU-hours per week`) as against a stock, `12 people`.
#
# Why the distinction is a relation and not an `IF`
#   "What fraction of the period is this supply available?" is a malformed question against a
#   stock: `12 people` has no period, so "five days" has nothing to be five days of. Against a
#   rate it is perfectly answerable, and `notApplicable` there is a refusal dressed as a
#   category error. The set of units that have a period is therefore the thing that decides
#   whether an absence is honest.
#
# A denominator is not a period
#   The name of this file is the predicate it tests. `LIKE '% per %' OR LIKE '% por %'` finds a
#   denominator; this rule needs a period, and the corpus separates them: `asrt:factor` files
#   `GPU-hour per GPU`, whose denominator is a GPU. At nameplate grain the two agree on every
#   filing here, so a relation can carry the wrong predicate and the right rows until somebody
#   files the case that splits them.
#
# The two editions stay out of the SQL entirely
#   `shifts per week` and `turnos por semana` both file `pm:denominator/pm:period`, so nothing
#   here decides that `semana` is `week`. Written as an `OR` over two spellings, that judgement
#   belongs to no filing.
#
# `owns` is a two-segment path, not an element name
#   That is what keeps this one row per layer. `Demand/amount` and `Nameplate/amount` are the
#   same element name, so a name-keyed `owns` makes this relation answer for the demand too and
#   return two rows per layer. A semi-join consumer cannot see the duplicate; a fault of that
#   shape sits here undetected until something joins on it. A duty cycle is a fraction of the
#   nameplate's period, and the demand's period is a different fact belonging to a different
#   question.
#
# The disagreeing layer already exists
#   So this is live rather than latent. `every-absence/delivery` quotes its demand `per day` and
#   files no nameplate amount at all, so a name-keyed predicate admits it on the strength of a
#   fact about demand. It costs nothing only because that layer's window is `unmeasured`, and
#   `window_not_applicable_on_a_rate` examines only `notApplicable` windows. One filing away
#   from a wrong verdict is not the same as safe.
-- pm:Claim/pm:denominator/pm:period, at pm:Nameplate/amount.
SELECT c.filing, c.layer, c.unit, c.denominator AS period
FROM      (
    :compose(epistemics/claims.sqlc)
) c
WHERE c.owns = 'pm:nameplate/pm:amount'
  AND c.denominator_kind = 'period'
